# PRD: Aretea Drive MCP Connector (v1 — read-only)

`Status:` Draft · `Last updated:` 2 July 2026

> A PRD says *what* and *why*, precisely enough to build from and to later judge whether it worked. It stops short of *how*.

---

## 1. Problem & context

Aretea's team wants to query a curated set of company documents from Claude web chat, where most of the team already works. The two off-the-shelf ways to connect Claude to Google Drive both fail: Claude's native Drive connector (and every managed gateway on a non-enterprise plan) uses **per-user OAuth**, so Claude inherits each individual's full personal Drive access — the wrong security boundary — while the gateways that support a shared admin credential (MintMCP, TrueFoundry) are enterprise-priced. After evaluating Composio, Nango, Docker MCP Gateway, and others, none satisfies {shared service-account credential + claude.ai web + curated corpus + EU processing + non-enterprise cost} at once. The viable path is a small self-hosted MCP server. This is also the first of several planned connectors (Slack, HubSpot, Granola), so the pattern established here matters beyond Drive.

## 2. Goal & success metric

Give all Claude Team web users **read** access to one admin-defined Google Drive corpus, governed by a single service account whose Drive membership — not application logic — is the security boundary, with no per-user Drive OAuth and no ongoing per-seat cost.

- **Primary:** the connector is in weekly active use within 4 weeks of launch. Measured informally — Frederik asks the team; no in-code usage tracking in v1. _Assumed timeframe — confirm or correct._
- **Guardrail:** zero instances of the connector reading any file outside the AI-Visible Shared Drive (Finance/HR/other drives never reachable), verified before launch and spot-checked after.
- **Secondary (strategic):** the server is structured so a second connector (Slack/HubSpot/Granola) can be added as an additional toolset without re-solving auth or hosting. _Not a launch metric, but a design acceptance check._

## 3. Scope

**In**
- [ ] One self-hosted MCP server exposing a single Google Drive corpus to claude.ai web, **read-only**.
- [ ] Service-account credential on the Drive hop; OAuth 2.1 on the Claude hop; the two decoupled.
- [ ] Read across the corpus: search, get file/metadata, and read content of Google Docs, Google Sheets, Google Slides, and regular files — at **basic text/value fidelity** (Doc text, Sheet cell values, Slide text-per-slide).
- [ ] Addable by a Team Owner as a custom connector; usable by **all** Team web users.
- [ ] Deployed in an EU region.
- [ ] Audit **logging** of tool calls (who-at-the-org, what tool, when) to a sink Aretea controls — logs only, no dashboards or alerting.

**Out (explicit)**
- **All write/edit operations** (create, update, rich Google Docs editing, delete, share, permission changes). v1 is read-only. Not built, not exposed. — *later PRD*
- **Higher read fidelity: Google Sheets formulas and Google Slides structure/layout.** v1 reads values and text only. — *PRD 2*
- **Monitoring, usage analytics, dashboards, alerting.** No in-code usage tracking; adoption is judged by asking the team. — *PRD 3*
- Per-user or per-group access differentiation. The connector is all-or-nothing at the org level (a platform limit, accepted): every Team user who enables it reaches the same corpus as the same service account. **Do not** build application-layer user gating — it would be a false boundary.
- Access to any Drive outside the single AI-Visible Shared Drive.
- Slack/HubSpot/Granola connectors (future; design must not preclude them, but they are not in v1).
- Claude Code / Desktop as the target surface (this is a claude.ai **web** connector; it will also work in Code/Desktop but that is not the requirement).

## 4. Requirements

**Functional** — _observable, testable behavior._
- [ ] FR1 — A Team Owner can add the server as a custom connector in claude.ai web and complete the OAuth 2.1 authorization; the connector shows as connected.
- [ ] FR2 — A non-admin Team user can enable the connector and use it **without being prompted to authorize their own Google account**.
- [ ] FR3 — Claude can search the corpus and return matching files from the AI-Visible drive.
- [ ] FR4 — Claude can read the content of a corpus file — Google Docs, Sheets (cell values), Slides (text), and regular files — at basic text/value fidelity.
- [ ] FR5 — A request that would touch a file outside the AI-Visible drive returns no data (the credential cannot reach it) — verified by a negative test against a known Finance/HR file.
- [ ] FR6 — The exposed tool list contains **only read tools**; no create, update, delete, share, or permission tool is present in the server's tool schema.

**Non-functional & constraints**
- [ ] NFR1 — **Boundary at the membership layer:** the service account is a member of only the AI-Visible Shared Drive. This is the primary control; tool scoping is defense-in-depth, never the sole boundary.
- [ ] NFR2 — **Two-hop auth, decoupled:** OAuth 2.1 (with DCR/PKCE, S256) satisfies the claude.ai web connector on the client hop; a service-account credential authenticates the Drive hop inside tool execution. Neither hop uses domain-wide delegation. The client-hop OAuth token's user identity is not used for data scoping (all users share the service-account view).
- [ ] NFR3 — **EU data processing:** the server runs in an EU region.
- [ ] NFR4 — **Publicly reachable from Anthropic's IP ranges** over HTTPS (claude.ai connects from Anthropic's cloud, not the user's device); not behind VPN/corporate firewall. May be locked down to Anthropic's published inbound ranges.
- [ ] NFR5 — **No standing per-seat cost and no third-party gateway** in the data path; self-hosted only.
- [ ] NFR6 — **Extensible to future toolsets:** auth/hosting structured so an additional connector is a new toolset behind the same OAuth front, not a new system.
- [ ] NFR7 — Secrets (service-account key, OAuth client secret) held in a secrets manager / 1Password, never committed.

## 5. Key flows

**Setup (one-time, admin)**
1. Create/confirm the AI-Visible Shared Drive → create the service account → add it as a **reader/viewer** member of that drive **only** → verify it is not a member of any other drive.
2. Deploy the MCP server (EU region) with the service-account credential and the OAuth client configured.
3. **Gate:** Owner adds the server URL in claude.ai web → connector completes OAuth handshake → shows connected. **If this fails, stop — this is the load-bearing step.**
4. Owner enables the connector org-wide.

**Use (per user)**
1. User enables the connector in a conversation → **no personal Google prompt** → asks Claude to find/read a corpus file → Claude calls the tool → result returned.

**Failure / edge cases**
- **OAuth handshake fails on add (FR1):** most likely a scope/DCR mismatch. This is the first verification gate; resolve before building tools. The claude.ai connector contract is documented (401 → Protected Resource Metadata → DCR/PKCE-S256, Streamable HTTP transport, callback `https://claude.ai/api/mcp/auth_callback`).
- **User sees a "connect your Google account" prompt (violates FR2):** means the Drive hop is leaking into the client hop — the service account is not the sole Drive credential. Blocker.
- **Cross-drive access returns data (violates FR5):** the service account has membership it shouldn't — fix at Drive membership, not in code.
- **Server unreachable from Anthropic IPs (violates NFR4):** connector silently fails to connect even though it works from the office.

